  "Name": "Warren1968-T6-HL4H-a200",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 6, p. 74; same stack (lossless) as Fig. 2 curve HLHLHLHLHS, p. 68",
  "Notes": "Quarter-wave reflecting stack (HL)^4H on S, 9 layers, all layers absorbing (alpha = 200 cm^-1); absorption at the reflection-band centre\nSpecification: Cut-on / cut-off reflection stack; absorption loss at lambda0/lambda = 1 (centre of the high-reflection band), lambda0 = 4 um.\nReported: Table 6 (p. 74): total absorption 0.63 % of incident radiation at lambda0/lambda = 1 (lambda0 = 4 um, alpha_H = alpha_L = 200 cm^-1). Per layer from air side, %: 0.249, 0.249, 0.051, 0.051, 0.01, 0.01, 0.0021, 0.0021, 0.0005. Fig. 2 (lossless, log scale): reflection band edge near lambda0/lambda 0.7 and 1.3 (mirror image about 1), transmission ripples in 0.3-0.7.\nNotes: Design string written out: HLHLHLHLHS. Both H and L absorb with alpha = 200 cm^-1 (k = 0.006366 at 4 um, constant). For the lossless Fig. 2 curves replace the materials by H n=4.0 (Warren 1968) / L n=1.8 (Warren 1968). H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised.\nLayers from Table 6, p. 74; same stack (lossless) as Fig. 2 curve HLHLHLHLHS, p. 68; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours A 0.624 % at 4 um; paper total absorption 0.63 % (Table 6).",
